  is a small change to an existing flow — I'll present a short design in chat rather than write a spec.

  One thing I need from you before I can write it: there's no [38;5;153muserId[39m anywhere in this app. The form collects only
  [38;5;153musername[39m and [38;5;153mpassword[39m ([38;5;153mindex.html[39m fields [38;5;153m#username[39m/[38;5;153m#password[39m), and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m already logs and returns the username as
  the identity. So a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter needs a source, and the options differ in what else has to change:

  - [1mCaller-supplied from a new form field[0m — add a [38;5;153muserId[39m input to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, read it in the submit handler, pass it
    through. Buys an explicit, user-provided id; costs a UI change and asks the user to type an id, which is unusual on
    a login form.
  - [1mCaller-supplied from client state[0m — the handler derives the id from something already on the client (a
    stored/session/analytics id) and passes it. No UI change, but there's no such state in the repo today, so we'd be
    inventing where it comes from.
  - [1mOptional parameter, default derived inside [38;5;153mlogin()[0m — signature becomes [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId = username)[39m.
    Nothing else must change, existing call keeps working, and callers that have a real id can pass one. Cheap to
    change later.

  The third is cheap to reverse; the first is the most expensive to undo because it touches the HTML and the user-facing
  form.

  My recommendation is the third — optional parameter with the username as the default — unless you have an actual id
  source in mind, in which case the second is right and I need to know where it comes from.
